\f0\fs24 \cf0 # AI Jobs Salary Prediction (2025\'962026)\
\
## Project Overview\
\
This project analyzes the AI job market dataset for the years 2025\'962026 and builds a machine learning model to predict salaries based on job-related features.\
\
The dataset contains information about job roles, experience levels, company size, industry, and country.\
\
The goal of this project is to explore patterns in the AI job market and create a predictive model that estimates salaries using machine learning techniques.\
\
---\
\
## Problem Type\
\
Supervised Machine Learning\
\
Category: **Regression**\
\
Why regression?\
\
Because the model predicts a **numeric value (salary)** instead of a category.\
\
---\
\
## Dataset Features\
\
The dataset includes features such as:\
\
- Job Title\
- Experience Level\
- Company Size\
- Industry\
- Country\
- Remote Work Type\
\
These features help the model understand patterns that influence salaries in the AI job market.\
\
---\
\
## Model Used\
\
Random Forest Regressor\
\
Random Forest is an ensemble learning algorithm that combines multiple decision trees to improve prediction accuracy.\
\
Advantages:\
- Handles complex relationships\
- Reduces overfitting\
- Good performance on structured datasets\
\
---\
\
## Evaluation Metrics\
\
The model performance is evaluated using the following regression metrics:\
\
**Mean Absolute Error (MAE)**  \
Measures the average difference between predicted and actual values.\
\
**Mean Squared Error (MSE)**  \
Measures the squared differences between predictions and real values.\
\
**R\'b2 Score**  \
Shows how well the model explains the variance in the data.\
\
Example code:\
\
```python\
from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, r2_score\
\
mae = mean_absolute_error(y_test, predictions)\
mse = mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions)\
r2 = r2_score(y_test, predictions)\
\
print("Mean Absolute Error:", mae)\
print("Mean Squared Error:", mse)\
print("R2 Score:", r2)}
